{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"derive-traits","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":240,"end_byte":274,"start_line":13,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":35}}],"id":"crate::domain::order::Order","kind":"Struct","name":"Order","qualified_name":"crate::domain::order::Order","parent":"ns:crate::domain::order","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":276,"end_byte":394,"start_line":14,"start_col":1,"end_line":19,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["derive:Debug","derive:Clone","derive:PartialEq","pub","impl:Priced","impl:Display"],"signature":"pub struct Order","doc":"A customer order. Immutable: changes create a copy.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" A customer order. Immutable: changes create a copy.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":183,"end_byte":240,"start_line":12,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"`pub` makes this declaration public. Code can access it through a path only when the containing modules and other parts of that path are also accessible. A public re-export can provide another accessible path. Public visibility of a type does not automatically make its fields public.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":276,"end_byte":279,"start_line":14,"start_col":1,"end_line":14,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Structure","text":"struct defines Order as a new type that groups related data. Each value has these named fields: id: u32, customer: String, amount: i64, status: OrderStatus. The type after each colon specifies the kind of value stored in that field. This declaration describes the shape of values; it does not create a value by itself.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":276,"end_byte":394,"start_line":14,"start_col":1,"end_line":19,"end_col":2}},{"group":"Derive Clone and Debug","text":"`#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` is an attribute on Order. It asks the compiler to apply the listed derive macros to this declaration and add the implementations they generate. If Clone and Debug resolve to the standard derives, the result includes Clone (explicit duplication via `.clone()`) and Debug (developer-oriented formatting such as `{:?}`). These derives may impose requirements on fields or type parameters; they do not automatically call clone or print anything. The written names alone do not prove which derive macros they resolve to.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":240,"end_byte":274,"start_line":13,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":35}},{"group":"Derive Debug","text":"This #[derive(...)] attribute asks the compiler to run the listed derive macro for Order. If Debug resolves to Rust's standard derive and expansion succeeds, it generates an implementation of the Debug formatting trait for this type. That implementation can be used by formats such as {:?} to produce developer-oriented text; its exact output is not a stable serialization format. Field types and generic parameters can impose requirements. The attribute does not itself print anything; formatting happens only when resulting code requests it. A custom derive can share this name, so the written spelling alone does not prove which implementation is generated.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":249,"end_byte":254,"start_line":13,"start_col":10,"end_line":13,"end_col":15}},{"group":"Derive Clone","text":"`#[derive(Clone)]` requests generated code for the following type during compilation. If `Clone` resolves to Rust's standard derive macro, it generates an implementation of the standard `Clone` trait; cloning a struct or enum clones its fields, and generic types may need corresponding bounds. The fields must support the required cloning operations. `clone()` is an explicit operation, not an automatic copy on every assignment, and a field's clone may share data rather than making an independent deep copy. `Clone` could refer to a different derive macro, so its spelling alone does not prove that standard behavior.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":240,"end_byte":274,"start_line":13,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":35}},{"group":"Outer attribute","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)] is an outer attribute: `#[...]` attaches attribute information to the declaration that follows it. Depending on the resolved attribute and context, it can supply metadata or affect compilation; an attribute macro can also transform the annotated syntax. The written name alone does not establish its meaning, whether it is accepted here, or a runtime action. Rust checks the attribute and its placement; `#![...]` instead applies to an enclosing item such as a module or crate.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":240,"end_byte":274,"start_line":13,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":35}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":183,"end_byte":186,"start_line":12,"start_col":1,"end_line":12,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Trait implementation","text":"This `impl Priced for Order` says that Order implements the Priced trait. A trait describes a set of behavior or associated items; this block supplies the required items that lack defaults and may override provided defaults for this type. Code can then use Order where Priced is required, subject to the trait's bounds and visibility. The declarations inside the braces are associated with this implementation; the block does not create a Order value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1095,"end_byte":1117,"start_line":49,"start_col":1,"end_line":49,"end_col":23}},{"group":"Trait implementation","text":"This `impl fmt::Display for Order` says that Order implements the fmt::Display trait. A trait describes a set of behavior or associated items; this block supplies the required items that lack defaults and may override provided defaults for this type. Code can then use Order where fmt::Display is required, subject to the trait's bounds and visibility. The declarations inside the braces are associated with this implementation; the block does not create a Order value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1251,"end_byte":1279,"start_line":59,"start_col":1,"end_line":59,"end_col":29}},{"group":"Inherent implementation","text":"`impl Order` adds methods and other associated items to the type Order. The braces contain their definitions; this block does not create a value. Methods with a `self` receiver operate on a value, while associated functions without one are called through the type, for example Player::new() for a Player implementation. The type and implementation can be declared separately.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":801,"end_byte":812,"start_line":39,"start_col":1,"end_line":39,"end_col":12}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":308,"end_byte":311,"start_line":15,"start_col":13,"end_line":15,"end_col":16}},{"group":"Signed integer type","text":"i64 is a signed 64-bit integer type. It represents whole numbers from -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 through 9,223,372,036,854,775,807, inclusive; it can hold negative values, zero, and positive values. The number after i is the number of bits, not a decimal digit count.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":357,"end_byte":360,"start_line":17,"start_col":17,"end_line":17,"end_col":20}},{"group":"Generated implementations","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":240,"end_byte":274,"start_line":13,"start_col":1,"end_line":13,"end_col":35}}]}
