rt_col":12,"end_line":60,"end_col":17}},{"group":"Trait implementation","text":"This `impl fmt::Display for Order` says that Order implements the fmt::Display trait. A trait describes a set of behavior or associated items; this block supplies the required items that lack defaults and may override provided defaults for this type. Code can then use Order where fmt::Display is required, subject to the trait's bounds and visibility. The declarations inside the braces are associated with this implementation; the block does not create a Order value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1251,"end_byte":1279,"start_line":59,"start_col":1,"end_line":59,"end_col":29}},{"group":"Exclusive reference","text":"&mut T is an exclusive reference to a value of type T. It borrows the value rather than taking ownership of it, and permits mutation through this reference. While the borrow is active, other access to the borrowed place is restricted; Rust checks the lifetime and aliasing rules. Reborrowing through this reference is possible, but the original reference is restricted while the reborrow is active.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1303,"end_byte":1326,"start_line":60,"start_col":22,"end_line":60,"end_col":45}},{"group":"Inferred lifetime","text":"'_ is an anonymous lifetime placeholder. The compiler infers the lifetime required at this position from the surrounding context and Rust's lifetime rules. It is not a named lifetime shared automatically with other occurrences, and it is not a runtime value or a request to keep data alive forever. The inferred lifetime still has to satisfy borrowing and validity requirements.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1323,"end_byte":1325,"start_line":60,"start_col":42,"end_line":60,"end_col":44}},{"group":"Receives","text":"Borrow the receiver for reading","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1293,"end_byte":1298,"start_line":60,"start_col":12,"end_line":60,"end_col":17},"type_info