{"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"Self","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"Self"},"id":"crate::domain::order::Order::new","kind":"Method","name":"new","qualified_name":"crate::domain::order::Order::new","parent":"crate::domain::order::Order","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":819,"end_byte":974,"start_line":40,"start_col":5,"end_line":42,"end_col":6}],"flavors":["pub","static"],"signature":"pub fn new(id: u32, customer: &str, amount: i64, status: OrderStatus) -> Self","type_text":"Self","has_flow":true,"rust_contract":[{"group":"Function","text":"fn declares a function named new. The parentheses contain its parameters, and the braces contain its body. Declaring it makes the function available. The body runs when the function is called.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":823,"end_byte":825,"start_line":40,"start_col":9,"end_line":40,"end_col":11}},{"group":"Function signature","text":"fn declares the function new. The parentheses list its inputs: id has type u32; customer has type &str; amount has type i64; status has type OrderStatus. In each input, the type follows the name or pattern after a colon. The arrow -> introduces the function's return type, Self.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":819,"end_byte":897,"start_line":40,"start_col":5,"end_line":40,"end_col":83}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"pub makes the function new public: callers can access it if they can reach it through an accessible module path. Public access also depends on the visibility of containing modules; a public re-export can provide another accessible path.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":819,"end_byte":822,"start_line":40,"start_col":5,"end_line":40,"end_col":8}},{"group":"Current type","text":"In this implementation, Self means Order, the type receiving the implementation. Capitalized Self is a type name. Here -> Self says the function returns that type. It is different from lowercase self, which is the method's receiver.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":892,"end_byte":896,"start_line":40,"start_col":78,"end_line":40,"end_col":82}},{"group":"Inherent implementation","text":"`impl Order` adds methods and other associated items to the type Order. The braces contain their definitions; this block does not create a value. Methods with a `self` receiver operate on a value, while associated functions without one are called through the type, for example Player::new() for a Player implementation. The type and implementation can be declared separately.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":801,"end_byte":812,"start_line":39,"start_col":1,"end_line":39,"end_col":12}},{"group":"String slice type","text":"Rust's built-in str is a dynamically sized sequence of bytes that must contain valid UTF-8 text. It has no fixed size known at compile time, so it is usually used behind a reference such as &str; that reference describes a borrowed portion of text and carries its length. str is not the same as String, which owns a growable buffer. The meaning of an unqualified name depends on name resolution if a name shadows the built-in type.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":850,"end_byte":853,"start_line":40,"start_col":36,"end_line":40,"end_col":39}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":834,"end_byte":837,"start_line":40,"start_col":20,"end_line":40,"end_col":23}},{"group":"Shared reference type","text":"&str is a shared reference: it borrows a value of type str instead of taking ownership. Several shared references can coexist. The reference must remain valid for its lifetime; while it is used, ordinary mutation of the borrowed data is restricted, though types with interior mutability can permit controlled changes. A reference does not by itself copy the underlying value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":849,"end_byte":853,"start_line":40,"start_col":35,"end_line":40,"end_col":39}},{"group":"Signed integer type","text":"i64 is a signed 64-bit integer type. It represents whole numbers from -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 through 9,223,372,036,854,775,807, inclusive; it can hold negative values, zero, and positive values. The number after i is the number of bits, not a decimal digit count.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":863,"end_byte":866,"start_line":40,"start_col":49,"end_line":40,"end_col":52}},{"group":"Borrowed string slice","text":"&str is a shared reference to a string slice: it lets code read UTF-8 text stored elsewhere without taking ownership of that text. The reference must not outlive the text it refers to. It points to a sequence of bytes, so byte positions need not correspond to characters and a slice must end at UTF-8 character boundaries. This is a borrowed view, rather than an owned, growable String.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":849,"end_byte":853,"start_line":40,"start_col":35,"end_line":40,"end_col":39}},{"group":"Receives","text":"id","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":830,"end_byte":837,"start_line":40,"start_col":16,"end_line":40,"end_col":23},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"UInt32"},"origin":"Declared","native":"u32"}},{"group":"Receives","text":"customer","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":839,"end_byte":853,"start_line":40,"start_col":25,"end_line":40,"end_col":39},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Indirect","pointer":false,"target":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"String"}},"origin":"Declared","native":"&str"}},{"group":"Receives","text":"amount","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":855,"end_byte":866,"start_line":40,"start_col":41,"end_line":40,"end_col":52},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"Int64"},"origin":"Declared","native":"i64"}},{"group":"Receives","text":"status","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":868,"end_byte":887,"start_line":40,"start_col":54,"end_line":40,"end_col":73},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"OrderStatus","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"OrderStatus"}},{"group":"Returns","text":"Value returned to the caller","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":892,"end_byte":896,"start_line":40,"start_col":78,"end_line":40,"end_col":82},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"Self","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"Self"}}]}
