{"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"Order","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"Order"},"id":"crate::domain::order::Order::with_status","kind":"Method","name":"with_status","qualified_name":"crate::domain::order::Order::with_status","parent":"crate::domain::order::Order","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":982,"end_byte":1088,"start_line":44,"start_col":5,"end_line":46,"end_col":6}],"flavors":["pub","&self"],"signature":"pub fn with_status(&self, status: OrderStatus) -> Order","type_text":"Order","has_flow":true,"rust_contract":[{"group":"Function","text":"fn declares a function named with_status. The parentheses contain its parameters, and the braces contain its body. Declaring it makes the function available. The body runs when the function is called.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":986,"end_byte":988,"start_line":44,"start_col":9,"end_line":44,"end_col":11}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"pub makes the function with_status public: callers can access it if they can reach it through an accessible module path. Public access also depends on the visibility of containing modules; a public re-export can provide another accessible path.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":982,"end_byte":985,"start_line":44,"start_col":5,"end_line":44,"end_col":8}},{"group":"Receiver","text":"self names the value on which this method is called, like this in C#. &self borrows that value for shared access while the borrow lasts.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1001,"end_byte":1006,"start_line":44,"start_col":24,"end_line":44,"end_col":29}},{"group":"Shared receiver","text":"&self means this method receives a shared reference to the value it was called on. The caller keeps ownership; the method borrows that value for access rather than taking it by value. Ordinary fields cannot be changed through this shared reference, although types with interior mutability can permit controlled changes. The reference remains valid only for its borrow's lifetime; multiple shared references can coexist while Rust's borrowing rules are satisfied.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1001,"end_byte":1006,"start_line":44,"start_col":24,"end_line":44,"end_col":29}},{"group":"Inherent implementation","text":"`impl Order` adds methods and other associated items to the type Order. The braces contain their definitions; this block does not create a value. Methods with a `self` receiver operate on a value, while associated functions without one are called through the type, for example Player::new() for a Player implementation. The type and implementation can be declared separately.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":801,"end_byte":812,"start_line":39,"start_col":1,"end_line":39,"end_col":12}},{"group":"Receives","text":"Borrow the receiver for reading","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1001,"end_byte":1006,"start_line":44,"start_col":24,"end_line":44,"end_col":29},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Indirect","pointer":false,"target":{"kind":"Named","name":"self","arguments":[]}},"origin":"Declared","native":"&self"}},{"group":"Receives","text":"status","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1008,"end_byte":1027,"start_line":44,"start_col":31,"end_line":44,"end_col":50},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"OrderStatus","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"OrderStatus"}},{"group":"Returns","text":"Value returned to the caller","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":1032,"end_byte":1037,"start_line":44,"start_col":55,"end_line":44,"end_col":60},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"Order","arguments":[]},"origin":"Declared","native":"Order"}}]}
{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"derive-traits","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":440,"end_byte":463,"start_line":22,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":24}}],"id":"crate::domain::order::OrderEvent","kind":"Union","name":"OrderEvent","qualified_name":"crate::domain::order::OrderEvent","parent":"ns:crate::domain::order","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":465,"end_byte":594,"start_line":23,"start_col":1,"end_line":27,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["derive:Debug","derive:Clone","pub"],"signature":"pub enum OrderEvent","doc":"Something that happened to an order.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" Something that happened to an order.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":398,"end_byte":440,"start_line":21,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"`pub` makes this declaration public. Code can access it through a path only when the containing modules and other parts of that path are also accessible. A public re-export can provide another accessible path. Public visibility of a type does not automatically make its fields public.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":465,"end_byte":468,"start_line":23,"start_col":1,"end_line":23,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Derive Clone and Debug","text":"`#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` is an attribute on OrderEvent. It asks the compiler to apply the listed derive macros to this declaration and add the implementations they generate. If Clone and Debug resolve to the standard derives, the result includes Clone (explicit duplication via `.clone()`) and Debug (developer-oriented formatting such as `{:?}`). These derives may impose requirements on fields or type parameters; they do not automatically call clone or print anything. The written names alone do not prove which derive macros they resolve to.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":440,"end_byte":463,"start_line":22,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":24}},{"group":"Derive Debug","text":"This #[derive(...)] attribute asks the compiler to run the listed derive macro for OrderEvent. If Debug resolves to Rust's standard derive and expansion succeeds, it generates an implementation of the Debug formatting trait for this type. That implementation can be used by formats such as {:?} to produce developer-oriented text; its exact output is not a stable serialization format. Field types and generic parameters can impose requirements. The attribute does not itself print anything; formatting happens only when resulting code requests it. A custom derive can share this name, so the written spelling alone does not prove which implementation is generated.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":449,"end_byte":454,"start_line":22,"start_col":10,"end_line":22,"end_col":15}},{"group":"Derive Clone","text":"`#[derive(Clone)]` requests generated code for the following type during compilation. If `Clone` resolves to Rust's standard derive macro, it generates an implementation of the standard `Clone` trait; cloning a struct or enum clones its fields, and generic types may need corresponding bounds. The fields must support the required cloning operations. `clone()` is an explicit operation, not an automatic copy on every assignment, and a field's clone may share data rather than making an independent deep copy. `Clone` could refer to a different derive macro, so its spelling alone does not prove that standard behavior.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":440,"end_byte":463,"start_line":22,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":24}},{"group":"Outer attribute","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone)] is an outer attribute: `#[...]` attaches attribute information to the declaration that follows it. Depending on the resolved attribute and context, it can supply metadata or affect compilation; an attribute macro can also transform the annotated syntax. The written name alone does not establish its meaning, whether it is accepted here, or a runtime action. Rust checks the attribute and its placement; `#![...]` instead applies to an enclosing item such as a module or crate.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":440,"end_byte":463,"start_line":22,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":24}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":398,"end_byte":401,"start_line":21,"start_col":1,"end_line":21,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Named-field enum variant","text":"Created is one possible variant of the OrderEvent enum. A value of the enum holds one variant at a time; when it is Created, it carries the data described inside these braces. Its named fields are id: u32. A colon separates each field name from the type of value it holds. This declaration describes the variant; it does not create a value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":492,"end_byte":511,"start_line":24,"start_col":5,"end_line":24,"end_col":24}},{"group":"Named-field enum variant","text":"Shipped is one possible variant of the OrderEvent enum. A value of the enum holds one variant at a time; when it is Shipped, it carries the data described inside these braces. Its named fields are id: u32, at: u64. A colon separates each field name from the type of value it holds. This declaration describes the variant; it does not create a value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":518,"end_byte":546,"start_line":25,"start_col":5,"end_line":25,"end_col":33}},{"group":"Named-field enum variant","text":"Cancelled is one possible variant of the OrderEvent enum. A value of the enum holds one variant at a time; when it is Cancelled, it carries the data described inside these braces. Its named fields are id: u32, reason: String. A colon separates each field name from the type of value it holds. This declaration describes the variant; it does not create a value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":553,"end_byte":590,"start_line":26,"start_col":5,"end_line":26,"end_col":42}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":506,"end_byte":509,"start_line":24,"start_col":19,"end_line":24,"end_col":22}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":532,"end_byte":535,"start_line":25,"start_col":19,"end_line":25,"end_col":22}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u64` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 64-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 18,446,744,073,709,551,615, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 64 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":541,"end_byte":544,"start_line":25,"start_col":28,"end_line":25,"end_col":31}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":569,"end_byte":572,"start_line":26,"start_col":21,"end_line":26,"end_col":24}},{"group":"Generated implementations","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone)]","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":440,"end_byte":463,"start_line":22,"start_col":1,"end_line":22,"end_col":24}}]}
