{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"derive-traits","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}}],"id":"crate::domain::order::OrderStatus","kind":"Enum","name":"OrderStatus","qualified_name":"crate::domain::order::OrderStatus","parent":"ns:crate::domain::order","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":99,"end_byte":179,"start_line":5,"start_col":1,"end_line":10,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["derive:Debug","derive:Clone","derive:Copy","derive:PartialEq","derive:Eq","pub"],"signature":"pub enum OrderStatus","doc":"Where an order is in its life.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" Where an order is in its life.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":17,"end_byte":53,"start_line":3,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"`pub` makes this declaration public. Code can access it through a path only when the containing modules and other parts of that path are also accessible. A public re-export can provide another accessible path. Public visibility of a type does not automatically make its fields public.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":99,"end_byte":102,"start_line":5,"start_col":1,"end_line":5,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Derive Clone and Debug","text":"`#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` is an attribute on OrderStatus. It asks the compiler to apply the listed derive macros to this declaration and add the implementations they generate. If Clone and Debug resolve to the standard derives, the result includes Clone (explicit duplication via `.clone()`) and Debug (developer-oriented formatting such as `{:?}`). These derives may impose requirements on fields or type parameters; they do not automatically call clone or print anything. The written names alone do not prove which derive macros they resolve to.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}},{"group":"Derive Debug","text":"This #[derive(...)] attribute asks the compiler to run the listed derive macro for OrderStatus. If Debug resolves to Rust's standard derive and expansion succeeds, it generates an implementation of the Debug formatting trait for this type. That implementation can be used by formats such as {:?} to produce developer-oriented text; its exact output is not a stable serialization format. Field types and generic parameters can impose requirements. The attribute does not itself print anything; formatting happens only when resulting code requests it. A custom derive can share this name, so the written spelling alone does not prove which implementation is generated.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":62,"end_byte":67,"start_line":4,"start_col":10,"end_line":4,"end_col":15}},{"group":"Derived Copy and Clone","text":"If Copy and Clone resolve to Rust's standard derive macros, this attribute asks the compiler to generate both trait implementations for the annotated type. Copy lets a by-value use copy the value's bits instead of moving the original; copying a reference or pointer does not duplicate its target. The derived Clone implementation explicitly clones the fields when clone is called. Copy requires Clone, all fields to be Copy, and the type not to implement Drop; generated implementations can also impose generic bounds. The written names can resolve differently, so the spelling alone does not prove these are the standard derives.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}},{"group":"Derive Clone","text":"`#[derive(Clone)]` requests generated code for the following type during compilation. If `Clone` resolves to Rust's standard derive macro, it generates an implementation of the standard `Clone` trait; cloning a struct or enum clones its fields, and generic types may need corresponding bounds. The fields must support the required cloning operations. `clone()` is an explicit operation, not an automatic copy on every assignment, and a field's clone may share data rather than making an independent deep copy. `Clone` could refer to a different derive macro, so its spelling alone does not prove that standard behavior.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}},{"group":"Outer attribute","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)] is an outer attribute: `#[...]` attaches attribute information to the declaration that follows it. Depending on the resolved attribute and context, it can supply metadata or affect compilation; an attribute macro can also transform the annotated syntax. The written name alone does not establish its meaning, whether it is accepted here, or a runtime action. Rust checks the attribute and its placement; `#![...]` instead applies to an enclosing item such as a module or crate.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":17,"end_byte":20,"start_line":3,"start_col":1,"end_line":3,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Enumeration","text":"enum defines OrderStatus as a type with these named alternatives, called variants: Pending, Paid, Shipped, Cancelled. A value of OrderStatus is exactly one variant at a time. These variants carry no additional fields; write OrderStatus::Pending to use the first one as a value. The declaration defines the choices; it does not create a value by itself.","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":99,"end_byte":179,"start_line":5,"start_col":1,"end_line":10,"end_col":2}},{"group":"Generated implementations","text":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]","span":{"file":"src/domain/order.rs","start_byte":53,"end_byte":97,"start_line":4,"start_col":1,"end_line":4,"end_col":45}}]}
