{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"trait-contract","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":31,"end_byte":36,"start_line":2,"start_col":5,"end_line":2,"end_col":10}}],"id":"crate::domain::shapes::Shape","kind":"Trait","name":"Shape","qualified_name":"crate::domain::shapes::Shape","parent":"ns:crate::domain::shapes","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":27,"end_byte":142,"start_line":2,"start_col":1,"end_line":7,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["pub"],"signature":"pub trait Shape","doc":"A shape with an area.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" A shape with an area.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":0,"end_byte":27,"start_line":1,"start_col":1,"end_line":2,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"`pub` makes this declaration public. Code can access it through a path only when the containing modules and other parts of that path are also accessible. A public re-export can provide another accessible path. Public visibility of a type does not automatically make its fields public.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":27,"end_byte":30,"start_line":2,"start_col":1,"end_line":2,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":0,"end_byte":3,"start_line":1,"start_col":1,"end_line":1,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Trait","text":"trait declares Shape as a set of behavior that types can implement. It describes what an implementing type provides; declaring the trait does not create a value. The method area has no body here, so each implementation must provide it. Its &self parameter borrows the value being used for shared access; it does not take ownership. The method name has a default body here, which an implementation may override. Its &self parameter borrows the value being used for shared access; it does not take ownership.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":27,"end_byte":142,"start_line":2,"start_col":1,"end_line":7,"end_col":2}}]}
