{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"trait-object","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":581,"end_byte":590,"start_line":32,"start_col":33,"end_line":32,"end_col":42}}],"value_info":{"kind":"Value","basis":"Syntax"},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"Float64"},"origin":"Declared","native":"f64"},"id":"crate::domain::shapes::total_area","kind":"Function","name":"total_area","qualified_name":"crate::domain::shapes::total_area","parent":"ns:crate::domain::shapes","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":549,"end_byte":648,"start_line":32,"start_col":1,"end_line":34,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["pub"],"signature":"pub fn total_area(shapes: &[Box<dyn Shape>]) -> f64","type_text":"f64","has_flow":true,"doc":"The area of every shape added up.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" The area of every shape added up.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":510,"end_byte":549,"start_line":31,"start_col":1,"end_line":32,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Function","text":"fn declares a function named total_area. The parentheses contain its parameters, and the braces contain its body. Declaring it makes the function available. The body runs when the function is called.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":553,"end_byte":555,"start_line":32,"start_col":5,"end_line":32,"end_col":7}},{"group":"Function signature","text":"fn declares the function total_area. The parentheses list its inputs: shapes has type &[Box<dyn Shape>]. In each input, the type follows the name or pattern after a colon. The arrow -> introduces the function's return type, f64.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":549,"end_byte":601,"start_line":32,"start_col":1,"end_line":32,"end_col":53}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"pub makes the function total_area public: callers can access it if they can reach it through an accessible module path. Public access also depends on the visibility of containing modules; a public re-export can provide another accessible path.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":549,"end_byte":552,"start_line":32,"start_col":1,"end_line":32,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":510,"end_byte":513,"start_line":31,"start_col":1,"end_line":31,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Slice type","text":"[Box<dyn Shape>] is a slice type: a contiguous sequence of elements of type Box<dyn Shape> whose length is not fixed in the type. Unlike [Box<dyn Shape>; N], it does not specify a number of elements. A slice is dynamically sized, so it is usually used behind a reference or pointer such as &[Box<dyn Shape>] or Box<[Box<dyn Shape>]>; those pointers carry the length needed to use the slice. The slice type itself does not say who owns the elements or whether they can be changed.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":576,"end_byte":592,"start_line":32,"start_col":28,"end_line":32,"end_col":44}},{"group":"Shared reference type","text":"&[Box<dyn Shape>] is a shared reference: it borrows a value of type [Box<dyn Shape>] instead of taking ownership. Several shared references can coexist. The reference must remain valid for its lifetime; while it is used, ordinary mutation of the borrowed data is restricted, though types with interior mutability can permit controlled changes. A reference does not by itself copy the underlying value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":575,"end_byte":592,"start_line":32,"start_col":27,"end_line":32,"end_col":44}},{"group":"Shared slice reference","text":"This is a shared reference to a slice of Box<dyn Shape>: a borrowed view of zero or more contiguous elements stored elsewhere. The slice length can vary at runtime; the reference carries access to the elements and their length. It does not own the elements, and it cannot outlive the data it borrows. Shared access normally allows reading but not mutation through this reference; types with interior mutability can have additional rules.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":575,"end_byte":592,"start_line":32,"start_col":27,"end_line":32,"end_col":44}},{"group":"Dynamic trait object","text":"dyn Shape is a trait object type. `dyn` hides the concrete implementing type and lets code use the trait's supported methods through runtime dispatch: the method implementation is selected for the concrete value at runtime. The trait must be dyn-compatible. A trait object is dynamically sized, so it is normally used behind a pointer such as a reference or Box; the pointer's form determines whether the value is borrowed or owned. The written trait name still needs normal name resolution.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":581,"end_byte":590,"start_line":32,"start_col":33,"end_line":32,"end_col":42}},{"group":"Boxed trait object","text":"dyn Shape names a trait object: the concrete value's type is hidden behind the listed trait and method calls through that trait use runtime dispatch. If Box resolves to Rust's standard Box, the Box owns the dynamically sized value in heap storage; moving the Box transfers that owning handle, and dropping it releases the owned value. The written name alone does not prove which Box type is in scope. Any lifetime or additional trait bounds still constrain the stored value.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":577,"end_byte":591,"start_line":32,"start_col":29,"end_line":32,"end_col":43}},{"group":"Floating-point type","text":"f64 is a 64-bit floating-point type for approximate real-number calculations. It has about 15–17 significant decimal digits of precision, not a fixed number of digits after the decimal point. Many decimal fractions cannot be represented exactly, so arithmetic can round; it also supports positive and negative infinity and NaN. Rust does not implicitly convert between f32 and f64.","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":597,"end_byte":600,"start_line":32,"start_col":49,"end_line":32,"end_col":52}},{"group":"Receives","text":"shapes","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":567,"end_byte":592,"start_line":32,"start_col":19,"end_line":32,"end_col":44},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Indirect","pointer":false,"target":{"kind":"Collection","family":"Slice","arguments":[{"kind":"Named","name":"Box","arguments":[{"kind":"Unknown"}]}]}},"origin":"Declared","native":"&[Box<dyn Shape>]"}},{"group":"Returns","text":"Value returned to the caller","span":{"file":"src/domain/shapes.rs","start_byte":597,"end_byte":600,"start_line":32,"start_col":49,"end_line":32,"end_col":52},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"Float64"},"origin":"Declared","native":"f64"}}]}
