ue at runtime. The trait must be dyn-compatible. A trait object is dynamically sized, so it is normally used behind a pointer such as a reference or Box; the pointer's form determines whether the value is borrowed or owned. The written trait name still needs normal name resolution.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":575,"end_byte":589,"start_line":23,"start_col":21,"end_line":23,"end_col":35}},{"group":"Boxed trait object","text":"dyn Fn(&Order) names a trait object: the concrete value's type is hidden behind the listed trait and method calls through that trait use runtime dispatch. If Box resolves to Rust's standard Box, the Box owns the dynamically sized value in heap storage; moving the Box transfers that owning handle, and dropping it releases the owned value. The written name alone does not prove which Box type is in scope. Any lifetime or additional trait bounds still constrain the stored value.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":571,"end_byte":590,"start_line":23,"start_col":17,"end_line":23,"end_col":36}},{"group":"Shared callable bound","text":"Fn(&Order) is a requirement on a callable type: it accepts arguments described by (&Order) and returns (). If Fn resolves to Rust's standard Fn trait, a call uses shared access to the callable instead of consuming it or requiring exclusive mutable access. Such a callable can be called repeatedly when each call completes; captured data may still change through interior mutability. The callable may be a closure or another implementing value. The written name alone does not establish its identity; the compiler checks that the concrete type meets this bound.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":579,"end_byte":589,"start_line":23,"start_col":25,"end_line":23,"end_col":35}}]}
