ces/order_service.rs","start_byte":769,"end_byte":810,"start_line":31,"start_col":1,"end_line":31,"end_col":42}},{"group":"Unit type","text":"() is the unit type. It has exactly one value, also written (). It commonly indicates that no meaningful result is returned; unlike the never type !, a function returning () can finish normally.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":2366,"end_byte":2368,"start_line":83,"start_col":47,"end_line":83,"end_col":49}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":2351,"end_byte":2354,"start_line":83,"start_col":32,"end_line":83,"end_col":35}},{"group":"May change","text":"Borrow the receiver with permission to change it","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":2336,"end_byte":2345,"start_line":83,"start_col":17,"end_line":83,"end_col":26},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Indirect","pointer":false,"target":{"kind":"Named","name":"self","arguments":[]}},"origin":"Declared","native":"&mut self"}},{"group":"Receives","text":"id","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":2347,"end_byte":2354,"start_line":83,"start_col":28,"end_line":83,"end_col":35},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Scalar","name":"UInt32"},"origin":"Declared","native":"u32"}},{"group":"Returns","text":"Value returned to the caller","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":2359,"end_byte":2380,"start_line":83,"start_col":40,"end_line":83,"end_col":61},"type_info":{"shape":{"kind":"Named","name":"Result","arguments":[{"kind":"Scalar","n