has no fixed size known at compile time, so it is usually used behind a reference such as &str; that reference describes a borrowed portion of text and carries its length. str is not the same as String, which owns a growable buffer. The meaning of an unqualified name depends on name resolution if a name shadows the built-in type.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":1219,"end_byte":1222,"start_line":45,"start_col":49,"end_line":45,"end_col":52}},{"group":"Shared reference type","text":"&str is a shared reference: it borrows a value of type str instead of taking ownership. Several shared references can coexist. The reference must remain valid for its lifetime; while it is used, ordinary mutation of the borrowed data is restricted, though types with interior mutability can permit controlled changes. A reference does not by itself copy the underlying value.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":1218,"end_byte":1222,"start_line":45,"start_col":48,"end_line":45,"end_col":52}},{"group":"Signed integer type","text":"i64 is a signed 64-bit integer type. It represents whole numbers from -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 through 9,223,372,036,854,775,807, inclusive; it can hold negative values, zero, and positive values. The number after i is the number of bits, not a decimal digit count.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":1227,"end_byte":1230,"start_line":45,"start_col":57,"end_line":45,"end_col":60}},{"group":"Borrowed string slice","text":"&str is a shared reference to a string slice: it lets code read UTF-8 text stored elsewhere without taking ownership of that text. The reference must not outlive the text it refers to. It points to a sequence of bytes, so byte positions need not correspond to characters and a slice must end at UTF-8 character boundaries. This is a borrowed view, rather than an owned, growable String.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":1218,"end_byte":1222,"start_l