{"syntax_sites":[{"key":"derive-traits","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":174,"end_byte":190,"start_line":8,"start_col":1,"end_line":8,"end_col":17}}],"id":"crate::services::order_service::ShipError","kind":"Union","name":"ShipError","qualified_name":"crate::services::order_service::ShipError","parent":"ns:crate::services::order_service","language":"rust","origins":[{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":192,"end_byte":254,"start_line":9,"start_col":1,"end_line":12,"end_col":2}],"flavors":["derive:Debug","pub","impl:Display"],"signature":"pub enum ShipError","doc":"Why an order could not ship.","rust_contract":[{"group":"Outer documentation equivalence","text":"`///` introduces an outer documentation comment for the following item. Its text after the three slashes is \" Why an order could not ship.\"; the space after the slashes, if written, is part of that text. Rust treats this as a `#[doc = ...]` attribute with that string value: for example, `/// text` corresponds to `#[doc = \" text\"]`. Rustdoc can render this as item documentation, commonly Markdown; it is not an instruction executed at runtime. This is distinct from `//!`, which documents the enclosing item.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":140,"end_byte":174,"start_line":7,"start_col":1,"end_line":8,"end_col":1}},{"group":"Visibility","text":"`pub` makes this declaration public. Code can access it through a path only when the containing modules and other parts of that path are also accessible. A public re-export can provide another accessible path. Public visibility of a type does not automatically make its fields public.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":192,"end_byte":195,"start_line":9,"start_col":1,"end_line":9,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Derive Debug","text":"This #[derive(...)] attribute asks the compiler to run the listed derive macro for ShipError. If Debug resolves to Rust's standard derive and expansion succeeds, it generates an implementation of the Debug formatting trait for this type. That implementation can be used by formats such as {:?} to produce developer-oriented text; its exact output is not a stable serialization format. Field types and generic parameters can impose requirements. The attribute does not itself print anything; formatting happens only when resulting code requests it. A custom derive can share this name, so the written spelling alone does not prove which implementation is generated.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":183,"end_byte":188,"start_line":8,"start_col":10,"end_line":8,"end_col":15}},{"group":"Outer attribute","text":"#[derive(Debug)] is an outer attribute: `#[...]` attaches attribute information to the declaration that follows it. Depending on the resolved attribute and context, it can supply metadata or affect compilation; an attribute macro can also transform the annotated syntax. The written name alone does not establish its meaning, whether it is accepted here, or a runtime action. Rust checks the attribute and its placement; `#![...]` instead applies to an enclosing item such as a module or crate.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":174,"end_byte":190,"start_line":8,"start_col":1,"end_line":8,"end_col":17}},{"group":"Outer documentation comment","text":"A line beginning with /// is an outer documentation comment for the following item. Rust treats its text as documentation metadata (like an outer #[doc = ...] attribute), rather than executing the text as code. Rustdoc normally renders the text as Markdown and can test eligible Rust code examples as documentation tests. Multiple doc-comment lines can contribute to the same item. //! instead documents the enclosing item; //// is an ordinary line comment.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":140,"end_byte":143,"start_line":7,"start_col":1,"end_line":7,"end_col":4}},{"group":"Enum alternatives","text":"`enum` defines ShipError as a type with alternative forms called variants. Each value is exactly one variant and contains the fields belonging to that variant. NotFound carries positional fields of types u32; NotPaid carries positional fields of types u32. The types in parentheses describe values supplied in that order; for example, a tuple-style variant is constructed as ShipError::Variant(values). Code can use `match` to choose behavior and extract data for each variant. This declaration defines the type; it does not create a value.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":192,"end_byte":254,"start_line":9,"start_col":1,"end_line":12,"end_col":2}},{"group":"Trait implementation","text":"This `impl fmt::Display for ShipError` says that ShipError implements the fmt::Display trait. A trait describes a set of behavior or associated items; this block supplies the required items that lack defaults and may override provided defaults for this type. Code can then use ShipError where fmt::Display is required, subject to the trait's bounds and visibility. The declarations inside the braces are associated with this implementation; the block does not create a ShipError value.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":258,"end_byte":290,"start_line":14,"start_col":1,"end_line":14,"end_col":33}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":227,"end_byte":230,"start_line":10,"start_col":14,"end_line":10,"end_col":17}},{"group":"Unsigned integer type","text":"When `u32` refers to Rust's built-in type, it is an unsigned 32-bit integer: it stores whole numbers from 0 through 4,294,967,295, inclusive. The `u` means unsigned (no negative values), and 32 is the number of bits. Arithmetic does not represent fractions, and overflow behavior depends on the operation and build settings. A user-defined type can shadow this name, so the spelling alone does not prove its meaning.","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":246,"end_byte":249,"start_line":11,"start_col":13,"end_line":11,"end_col":16}},{"group":"Generated implementations","text":"#[derive(Debug)]","span":{"file":"src/services/order_service.rs","start_byte":174,"end_byte":190,"start_line":8,"start_col":1,"end_line":8,"end_col":17}}]}
